{"version":1,"slug":"seasons","topic":"Why do we have seasons?","level":"beginner","title":"Why Do We Have Seasons?","summary":"You will understand how Earth's tilt changes the angle of sunlight, creating summer and winter.","scopeNote":null,"scenes":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","title":"Why do we have seasons?","segments":[{"id":"intro-0","narration":"Many people think summer happens when Earth is closer to the sun, but that is actually backwards. So what really causes the seasons?","sig":"-Xjv4LyA0MA7GspzCuWWapMI1uOL0tMi","actions":[{"id":"hero","kind":"icon","cue":"Many people think","text":"Earth's seasons","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"intro-1","narration":"Here's our plan. First, It Is Not Distance. Then, How Tilt Changes Sunlight. And finally, Opposite Seasons on Earth.","sig":"IJ7C_goj664rmYRfbB8JwSEXLnJnAPuw","actions":[{"id":"agenda","kind":"list","cue":"Here's our plan","text":null,"items":["It Is Not Distance","How Tilt Changes Sunlight","Opposite Seasons on Earth"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c1","kind":"chapter","title":"It Is Not Distance","segments":[{"id":"c1-0","narration":"A lot of people think summer happens when Earth swings closer to the Sun, and winter happens when we are farther away. That sounds logical, but it is actually not true.","sig":"HqtNmIkrztkqMGbfk13ZeoLWC4obm2eT","actions":[{"id":"wrong","kind":"box","cue":"summer happens when","text":"Closer = Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":4,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"closer to the Sun","text":"Sun","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"x1","kind":"cross_out","cue":"actually not true","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"wrong","color":"red","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-1","narration":"In fact, Earth's orbit is nearly a circle, and we are actually closest to the Sun in January. Yet January is winter in the north.","sig":"Aw3FtIAOqQ-p0O-zD46hjwFoWAdbo9oW","actions":[{"id":"orbit","kind":"note","cue":"nearly a circle","text":"Orbit is nearly a circle","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":0,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"jan","kind":"callout","cue":"closest to the Sun","text":"Closest in January","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"sun","color":"purple","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-2","narration":"The real reason for seasons is that Earth spins on a tilted axis, leaning about twenty-three and a half degrees.","sig":"E7hAYQP6tPT_t2vLrUrjrkaDAg3ULpCv","actions":[{"id":"earth","kind":"icon","cue":"Earth spins on","text":"Earth (23.5° tilt)","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":7,"row":1,"w":3,"h":3},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"tilt","kind":"box","cue":"a tilted axis","text":"Tilted Axis","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":4,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-3","narration":"That steady tilt is what creates our seasons as we travel around the Sun.","sig":"G0c-gc7FcCJ2ppDaNEkSWApXkpKZ6oXd","actions":[{"id":"c1","kind":"check","cue":"steady tilt","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"tilt","color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"around the Sun","text":"Orbits","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"earth","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c2","kind":"chapter","title":"How Tilt Changes Sunlight","segments":[{"id":"c2-0","narration":"Now that you know Earth's tilt is the real reason, let's see how that tilt changes the sunlight.","sig":"HqMvz1-c6n2muxM-9XXwQMZO8kr1RisQ","actions":[{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"changes the sunlight","text":"Sunlight","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":1,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-1","narration":"When sunlight hits the ground straight on, all its energy packs into a small spot, making it warm.","sig":"EK_jEmLUVfWkL8x-lRqIKAgNVzt_Z0ZU","actions":[{"id":"direct","kind":"box","cue":"hits the ground straight","text":"Direct Hit","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":0,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"hits the ground straight","text":"concentrated","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"direct","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"warm","kind":"note","cue":"making it warm","text":"Small spot = Warm","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":0,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-2","narration":"When that same sunlight arrives at an angle, it spreads out over a much wider area, so it feels cooler.","sig":"SWAmgHaGbrzPZ3MQVW7bHZgAHFc-gtQw","actions":[{"id":"spread","kind":"box","cue":"arrives at an angle","text":"Spread Out","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":4,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a2","kind":"arrow","cue":"arrives at an angle","text":"angled","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"spread","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"cool","kind":"note","cue":"feels cooler","text":"Wide area = Cool","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":4,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-3","narration":"Think of a flashlight: pointing it straight down makes a bright, hot circle, while tilting it dims the glow.","sig":"Nlj8tnI08i2p_-P4s1J1xLtGTb8DCdvD","actions":[{"id":"hl1","kind":"highlight","cue":"bright, hot circle","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"warm","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"hl2","kind":"highlight","cue":"dims the glow","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"cool","color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c3","kind":"chapter","title":"Opposite Seasons on Earth","segments":[{"id":"c3-0","narration":"Now that you know how tilt concentrates sunlight, let us see what happens across the whole planet. Place the Sun in the middle, and picture Earth on one side in June.","sig":"-RseNzIbi74v7OLOHV-gNULyG_WcKrUp","actions":[{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"Place the Sun","text":"Sun","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":5,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"earth_june","kind":"icon","cue":"Earth on one side","text":"June","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":1,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a_june","kind":"arrow","cue":"in June","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"earth_june","target":null,"color":null,"expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-1","narration":"In June, the Northern Hemisphere tilts toward the Sun for summer, while the Southern Hemisphere tilts away into winter.","sig":"Vx0AKNy7SR8ZOqAJV-1SQSmhye3ggxBT","actions":[{"id":"june_north","kind":"box","cue":"Northern Hemisphere tilts","text":"North: Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":0,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"june_south","kind":"box","cue":"Southern Hemisphere tilts","text":"South: Winter","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":4,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-2","narration":"Six months later, Earth reaches the opposite side in December. Now the North tilts away into winter, and the South enjoys summer.","sig":"lJjHmHV52115Ou0cSHPUt_pegXKMXZH7","actions":[{"id":"earth_dec","kind":"icon","cue":"opposite side","text":"December","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":9,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a_dec","kind":"arrow","cue":"in December","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"earth_dec","target":null,"color":null,"expr":null},{"id":"dec_north","kind":"box","cue":"North tilts away","text":"North: Winter","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":8,"row":0,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"dec_south","kind":"box","cue":"South enjoys summer","text":"South: Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":8,"row":4,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-3","narration":"That is why the two hemispheres always have opposite seasons.","sig":"3WGpGzGY-8qYuJXRjsSOUNAUjOOGeLYA","actions":[{"id":"hl_june","kind":"highlight","cue":"opposite seasons","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"june_north","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"hl_dec","kind":"highlight","cue":"opposite seasons","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"dec_south","color":"orange","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"Recap","segments":[{"id":"recap-0","narration":"So remember: Earth's tilt causes seasons, not distance. Direct sunlight warms, angled sunlight cools, and opposite hemispheres swap seasons. Got any questions about how this works?","sig":"5GJ4Z-WuE7Q5WzBxEg4tEcHXxVgBrB0S","actions":[{"id":"earth","kind":"icon","cue":"Earth's tilt causes","text":"Seasons","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"takeaways","kind":"list","cue":"seasons, not distance","text":null,"items":["Earth's tilt causes seasons","Direct light warms, angled cools","Hemispheres have opposite seasons"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null}]}]}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why is it winter in January in the Northern Hemisphere even though Earth is closest to the Sun?","options":["Earth's axis is tilted away","The Sun is cooler then","The Moon blocks the heat","Oceans absorb all the warmth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Seasons are caused by Earth's tilt, not its distance from the Sun.","chapter":1},{"question":"Why does sunlight feel warmer when it hits the ground straight on?","options":["It travels much faster","It packs into a smaller spot","It has more oxygen","It reflects off clouds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Direct sunlight concentrates its energy into a smaller area, whereas angled sunlight spreads out.","chapter":2},{"question":"When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, what season is it in the Southern Hemisphere?","options":["Summer","Spring","Winter","Autumn"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because Earth's tilt points one hemisphere toward the Sun and the other away, they always have opposite seasons.","chapter":3}],"suggestions":["Why do days get longer?","What causes solar eclipses?","How does the Moon's orbit work?"],"models":{"lesson":"google/gemini-4-hydrogen","voice":"openai/gpt-audio-mini (marin)"},"createdAt":"2026-11-17T07:59:06.832Z","audioBase":"/samples/seasons"}